// FEA-in-the-loop design optimisation — a cantilever mounting bracket whose
// thickness is chosen BY THE SOLVER, not by the author.
//
// This is the end-to-end demonstration of a design loop that closes through a
// real finite-element analysis:
//
//   - `param thickness : Length = auto(free)` — a free design variable. Nothing
//     in this file assigns it a number; the constraint solver does.
//   - `minimize mass` — the objective. Mass is a genuine closed-form function of
//     the design variable, so it actually pulls thickness DOWN.
//   - a von-Mises stress `constraint` built on a real `solve_elastic_static`
//     call — the opposing force. It pushes thickness UP.
//
// The resolved thickness is where those two meet: the thinnest bracket whose
// peak stress still sits under the material's yield limit. Every trial thickness
// the optimiser proposes runs a real FEA solve — the analysis is a ComputeNode
// inside the dependency graph, evaluated inside the solver's cost loop, not a
// post-hoc report on an already-fixed design (wired by task 4880).
//
// The eval-layer convergence contract is pinned in
// `crates/reify-eval/tests/harness_fea_solver_e2e/fea_bracket_minimize_mass_e2e.rs`;
// the compile-surface contract in
// `crates/reify-compiler/tests/harness_compilation_surface/fea_bracket_minimize_mass_example.rs`.
//
// ── Why the stress predicate is a separate `constraint` member ───────────────
//
// The grammar also accepts `minimize <expr> where <cond>`, and it is tempting to
// write the whole design loop as one line. Do not. The parser lowers that guard
// into `MinimizeDecl.where_clause`, but the compiler's Minimize/Maximize lowering
// arms never read that field (every other member kind routes its guard through
// `compile_per_decl_guard`; these two do not). The predicate is silently
// DISCARDED — the structure compiles clean with zero constraints, the optimiser
// runs unopposed, and `thickness` collapses to the ~1 micron auto-param lower
// bound while still reporting "solved". A separate `constraint` member is the
// only form the compiler actually honours.
//
// ── Sizing basis (closed-form, so the numbers are auditable) ─────────────────
//
// Geometry is a prismatic plate: coordinates run [0, length] x [0, width] x
// [0, thickness], clamped at the root (x_min) face, carrying a point load `P`
// down the -Z axis at the free end. That is an end-loaded cantilever, so
// Euler-Bernoulli gives the peak fibre stress at the root:
//
//     M_max  = P * length                    (root bending moment)
//     S      = width * thickness^2 / 6       (section modulus)
//     sigma  = M_max / S = 6 * P * length / (width * thickness^2)
//
// Inverting at sigma = yield gives the thickness the design loop should find:
//
//     thickness* = sqrt(6 * P * length / (width * yield))
//                = sqrt(6 * 750 N * 0.05 m / (0.03 m * 310e6 Pa))
//                = sqrt(2.419e-5) = 4.9 mm
//
// `P = 750 N` is chosen to place that crossing at ~4.9 mm: two orders of
// magnitude above the 1 micron lower bound and three below the 10 m upper bound
// of the default `Length` auto-param range, and a clear factor of ~2 away from
// the solver's ~10 mm default seed, so a converged value cannot be confused with
// an unmoved starting point. The seed itself is comfortably feasible
// (sigma(10 mm) = 6 * 750 * 0.05 / (0.03 * 1e-4) = 75 MPa, a 4.1x margin under
// the 310 MPa yield), which lets the solver take its `initially_feasible` fast
// path.
//
// The 50 mm x 30 mm footprint matches the FEA-in-the-loop producer fixture
// (`fea_in_the_loop_producer.rs`), keeping the per-candidate mesh cost known.
//
// Beam theory is the SIZING BASIS, not an assertion: the FEA solve on a coarse
// tet mesh of a plate this stubby will not reproduce it exactly, and it is not
// expected to. Its job is to put the stress/yield crossing in a sane place.
// MEASURED: the loop converges at 4.52 mm, 8% below the 4.9 mm estimate — close
// enough to confirm the constraint binds at the stress/yield crossing rather
// than anywhere incidental, and NOT a threshold anything asserts against.

structure FeaBracketMinimizeMass {
    // ── Geometry ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // `length` and `width` are fixed by the mounting envelope. `thickness` is
    // the one free variable.
    param length    : Length = 50mm
    param width     : Length = 30mm

    // `auto(free)` rather than strict `auto`: strict `auto` adds a
    // perturbation-based uniqueness re-solve (a second full Nelder-Mead run),
    // roughly DOUBLING the number of real FEA solves. The design loop here is
    // single-variable and monotone — thinner is lighter, thinner is more
    // stressed — so the stress-limited optimum is unique by construction and the
    // re-solve buys no signal for a large runtime cost.
    param thickness : Length = auto(free)

    let material = Steel_AISI_1045()

    // Geometry realization is deferred — no kernel call at eval time.
    let body = box(length, width, thickness)

    // ── Loads and supports ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // A 750 N service load at the free end, bending the bracket about its weak
    // axis. `PointLoad.direction` defaults to vec3(0, 0, -1) — straight down the
    // -Z axis, i.e. across the thickness — which is the canonical bending case
    // and the one the sizing basis above assumes.
    //
    // WHY A POINT LOAD AND NOT A `PressureLoad` ON A NAMED FACE: a distributed
    // pressure would be the nicer physical demo, but neither spelling of its
    // `face` field survives an auto param today, and both fail SILENTLY.
    // Measured on this very file:
    //
    //   - `face: face(body, "z_max")` (the modern typed-selector form) evaluates
    //     the `pressure` cell to `{ "magnitude": Real(1000000.0), "face": Undef }`
    //     — the hoisted selector mints `Value::Undef` instead of a
    //     `Selector(Leaf { query: Named("z_max") })`, so `parse_pressure_spec`
    //     reads no face name and DROPS the load. The design loop then runs
    //     unopposed and thickness collapses to 2.88e-6 m, its lower bound. The
    //     control is decisive: the identical spelling in
    //     `examples/fea_pressure_smoke.ri`, whose box dims are all concrete,
    //     mints a real selector. The difference is the unresolved `auto`
    //     thickness feeding `box(...)`.
    //   - `face: "z_max"` (the legacy String arm) DOES apply the load and does
    //     converge — but `PressureLoad.face` is typed `Option<FaceSelector>`, so
    //     it trips the ctor-field-conformance corpus gate as a true positive.
    //
    // Filed as follow-up ticket tkt_0RSVYHGPGEYD2QV9MTGTXPQ86S. Until it lands,
    // `PointLoad.point` is a plain `String` by declaration — no selector to mint
    // and no conformance debt — which keeps this example honest end-to-end.
    let tip_load = PointLoad(point: "tip", force: 750.0)

    // "root" is the face name the synthetic cantilever clamp honours
    // (`SYNTHETIC_CLAMP_FACE`, elastic_static.rs) — the x_min end.
    let mount = FixedSupport(target: "root")

    // Mirrors `Steel_AISI_1045.yield_stress = some(310MPa)`. A plain Pressure
    // literal rather than the field itself: `yield_stress` is `Option<Pressure>`
    // and Reify has no `.unwrap()` / `?` operator, nor any `match some(x)`
    // precedent in the stdlib .ri files.
    let yield_limit = 310MPa

    // ── Objective ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // Closed-form mass. `length` and `width` are fixed, so this is linear and
    // strictly increasing in `thickness` — a real gradient for the optimiser to
    // descend, and a real Scalar<Mass> any downstream consumer can read.
    //
    // Written out rather than reached for via the `Physical` trait's derived
    // `mass = volume(geometry) * material.density`: that trait is Phase-1
    // typecheck-only today and evaluates to Undef, which would leave the
    // objective with nothing to minimise.
    let mass = material.density * length * width * thickness

    // ── Design predicate ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // Peak von-Mises stress anywhere in the body must stay under yield — the
    // field reduction this demo exists to show. `.stress` is a Field of rank-2
    // stress tensors sampled over the body; the peak von-Mises value is the
    // single worst scalar in it.
    //
    // That reduction is read here through the result's own `max_von_mises`
    // member rather than spelled out as `max(von_mises(result.stress))`. The two
    // compute the identical quantity — `max_von_mises` is the element-max von
    // Mises over exactly the same solid stress field — so the reduction still
    // genuinely happens; only the spelling differs.
    //
    // The explicit spelling is currently blocked by a COMPILE-TIME TYPING GAP,
    // not by a semantic one: `analysis_fn_result_type` derives von_mises's
    // dimension via `tensor_quantity`, which matches `Type::Tensor`/`Type::Matrix`
    // but has no `Type::Field` arm. A Field-valued argument therefore falls to
    // the DIMENSIONLESS default, `von_mises(.stress)` types as `Real`, and
    // `< yield_limit` fails with "dimension mismatch in comparison: Real vs
    // Scalar[kg·m^-1·s^-2]" — an Error, so the zero-Error examples gate rejects
    // it. Filed as follow-up ticket tkt_0RSVXM8TTBE200NJ7HD8BH24Q2; when that
    // lands, this constraint can move to the explicit form unchanged in meaning.
    //
    // The FEA call is INLINED here rather than bound to a top-level
    // `let fea = solve_elastic_static(...)`. That is not a style choice. A
    // `let`-bound `@optimized` call is eagerly dispatched by the engine's own
    // top-level ComputeNode pass as soon as its declaration is reached — BEFORE
    // the constraint solver has assigned `thickness` a numeric value — feeding
    // the trampoline an Undef dimension and panicking in `extract_scalar_si`.
    // Inlining keeps the call inside the constraint's compiled expression tree,
    // which only the solver's cost loop evaluates, with a numeric trial
    // thickness substituted on every candidate.
    //
    // `ShellForce.Off` pins the tet/solid path. Under the default
    // `ShellForce.Auto` the thickness/in-plane ratio decides between the solid
    // and shell formulations — and the optimiser moves thickness across three
    // orders of magnitude, so that classification would flip mid-solve and the
    // constraint would change meaning underneath the search. `ElasticOptions` is
    // passed explicitly because fn-param defaults for same-module struct
    // constructors are not supported.
    constraint solve_elastic_static(
        material, length, width, thickness, [tip_load], [mount],
        ElasticOptions(shell_force: ShellForce.Off)
    ).max_von_mises < yield_limit

    minimize mass
}
